## 7. Cash Flow and Quality of Earnings — The Most Important Section

| Period | Operating CF | Investing CF | Financing CF | Capex | FCF (OCF−Capex) | OCF / Net Income |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| FY2024 (annual) | 28,090 | −10,566 | −13,633 | 1,069 | 27,021 | 94.4% |
| FY2025 (annual) | 64,089 | −20,421 | −42,359 | 3,236 | 60,853 | 87.9% |
| **FY2026 (annual)** | **102,718** | **−52,228** | **−48,474** | **6,042** | **96,676** | **85.5%** |
| H1 FY2026 (6M) | 42,779 | −12,343 | −27,386 | 3,122 | 39,657 | **94.7%** |
| **H1 FY2027 (6M)** | **74,421** | **−35,124** | **−27,459** | **4,434** | **69,987** | **63.1%** |
| Q1 FY2027 | 50,344 | −26,429 | −21,283 | 1,757 | 48,587 | 86.3% |
| Q2 FY2027 | 24,077 | −8,695 | −6,176 | 2,677 | 21,400 | 40.3% |

### Three findings that matter

**(a) Cash conversion has deteriorated sharply — but the cause is likely timing, not receivables.**
H1 FY2027 operating cash flow of $74.4B covers only 63.1% of net income of $118.0B, versus 94.7% coverage in H1 FY2026. Normally this would be a red flag for aggressive revenue recognition. However, the balance sheet **contradicts** a receivables-blowout thesis: ex-cash current assets as a multiple of quarterly revenue actually *improved* from 1.94x to 1.82x. The most consistent explanation is **normalization of customer prepayments/deferred revenue and payables timing** (total liabilities grew +125% y/y, faster than the +106% revenue growth — accruals and customer deposits are expanding). Q2 FY2027's 40.3% conversion ratio is also seasonally typical (Q2 FY2026 was 15,365/26,422 = 58%; the Q1→Q2 pattern shows big customer prepayments landing in Q1).

**Actionable:** This is a *watch item*, not yet a sell signal. Track the next two quarters. A full year below 70% conversion with flat or rising DSO would convert this from timing to quality-of-earnings risk.

**(b) Investing outflows are now a first-order cash consumer.**
$35.1B deployed in investing activities in H1 FY2027 — **47% of operating cash flow** — versus $12.3B a year earlier. Only $4.4B of that is capex (NVDA is fabless; it owns almost no fabs). The remaining ~$30.7B is external investment: ecosystem equity stakes, supply-chain/partner investments, and capacity prepayments. FCF of $70.0B against $118.0B of net income means **~41% of reported earnings is not reaching free cash flow**. Watch this for (i) impairment risk if AI-adjacent valuations reset, and (ii) **circularity risk** — if NVDA is investing in the same customers buying its systems, revenue quality is structurally weaker than the headline suggests. This is the single most under-appreciated fundamental risk in the current numbers.

**(c) Capex intensity remains trivially low (4.4% of revenue in H1 FY2027), confirming the asset-light model.**
But note that the real capacity commitments are **off-balance-sheet purchase obligations and prepayments**, which are not captured in this dataset. The $30B+ of "investing" outflow is partly a proxy for those commitments. Capital intensity is therefore understated by the pure capex line.

