### 1. Regime classification: range-bound equilibrium inside a rising primary trend

The single most informative fact in this dataset is a **price magnet at ~217.50**. Five separate sessions closed within 12 cents of each other:

| Date | Close | Volume |
|---|---|---|
| 2026-08-10 | 217.55 | 115.8M |
| 2026-08-11 | 217.50 | 101.3M |
| 2026-08-19 | 217.56 | 96.8M |
| 2026-08-28 | 217.55 | 195.1M |
| 2026-09-01 | 217.44 | 109.8M |

That is roughly **600M shares transacted at the same price** — a genuine high-volume node / value area, not a coincidence. Price is being repeatedly auctioned back to it. **2026-09-01's close of 217.44 sits 0.03% below the 10 EMA (217.51) and 0.61% below the 20 SMA (218.78).** A market that keeps re-pricing to the same level while sitting essentially *on* its 10 EMA is a **mean-reverting, not trending, market on the daily timeframe** — even though the primary trend is unambiguously up.

### 2. Trend structure: bullish hierarchy, but compressed and stalling at the top of the stack

Moving-average stack as of 2026-09-01:

- **200 SMA: 195.70** — rising from 192.84 on 2026-08-03 (+2.86 over 21 sessions, ~+0.136/session)
- **50 SMA: 208.56** — rising from 205.56 on 2026-08-03 (+3.00, ~+0.143/session)
- **20 SMA (boll middle): 218.78** — rising sharply from 203.62 on 2026-08-03
- **10 EMA: 217.51** — peaked at 219.50 on 2026-08-17, has flattened and rolled mildly over

Reading the stack:

- **50 SMA (208.56) > 200 SMA (195.70)** with a spread of **12.86 points (6.6% of the 200 SMA)**. Both are rising at nearly identical rates, so the spread is *stable-to-slightly-widening* — this is a healthy, intact primary uptrend, not a decaying one. I can confirm the *configuration* as of the snapshot window; I **cannot** date the original golden cross, since indicator history is truncated to 30 days.
- **Price is +11.1% above the 200 SMA and +4.3% above the 50 SMA.** Constructive, and notably *not* extended — with ATR at 3.38% of price, NVDA is only **~1.2 ATR above its 50 SMA**. That matters for risk: the medium-term trend line is within one ordinary day's range, i.e. a normal pullback could retest it.
- **The short-term stack is deteriorating:** the 10 EMA (217.51) is now *below* the 20 SMA (218.78), and has been for four consecutive sessions (Aug 27: 216.84 vs 216.96; Sep 1: 217.51 vs 218.78). Price is above both, but the fast/slow short-term relationship has flipped. This is the classic signature of a **rally losing its thrust**, not of a collapse.

**Structural read of 2026:** the dataset traces a large **converging triangle** — descending major highs (236.54 on 2026-05-14 → 232.28 on 2026-06-02 → 230.47 on 2026-08-27) against ascending major lows (164.27 on 2026-03-30 → 189.80 on 2026-06-29 → 190.01 on 2026-07-29 → 207.25 on 2026-08-24). That is a six-month coiling structure with an apex in the **late-Q3/early-Q4** window. NVDA closed 2026-09-01 **8.1% below its 2026 closing high of 235.74 (2026-05-14)** and is still **+15.1% YTD** (188.85 → 217.44) and **+31.7% off the 2026-03-30 close of 165.17**.

### 3. Momentum: RSI is neutral — and quietly diverging

RSI at **51.87** on 2026-09-01 is dead-center neutral. The more valuable signal is in its trajectory:

- 2026-08-07 close **223.96** → RSI **64.42**
- 2026-08-17 close **225.01** → RSI **62.85**
- 2026-08-27 close **227.98** → RSI **61.32**

**Successively higher price peaks paired with successively lower RSI peaks — a clean three-point bearish divergence, fully supported by dated values.** Momentum is decelerating into each new high. This is *not* an overbought condition (nothing near 70), so it is not a short signal on its own; it is a **warning that upside breakouts are losing conviction**, which the failed 2026-08-27 breakout then confirmed.

The RSI path also shows how violent the intra-range chop is: RSI fell to **44.65 on 2026-08-24**, spiked to **61.32 on 2026-08-27**, and is back to **51.87**. That 16-point round trip in four sessions is not a trend characteristic; it is noise within a box. With MACD unavailable, RSI's divergence is my primary momentum-vs-price cross-check, and it says: **upside momentum is thinning.**

### 4. Volatility: an active squeeze is building — this is the actionable setup

This is where the Bollinger data earns its place in the set:

- Bandwidth **2026-08-26: 32.22 points = 14.96%** of the 20 SMA (UB 231.42 / LB 199.20)
- **2026-08-27: 12.9%** → **08-28: 10.8%** → **08-31: 9.69%** → **2026-09-01: 20.29 points = 9.27%** (UB 228.92 / LB 208.63)

**Five consecutive sessions of bandwidth contraction, cutting the envelope by roughly 38%.** The bands are now 208.63–228.92. `%B` on 2026-09-01 is **0.434** — price in the lower-middle of the envelope.

Confirming the compression from a second, independent volatility measure: **ATR 7.34** (3.38% of price). ATR had *declined steadily* from 7.87 (2026-08-05) to **6.25 (2026-08-26)** — a 21% contraction — then **re-expanded to 7.65 on 2026-08-28** after the 2026-08-27 spike, and is now easing again. Realized daily ranges tell the same story: **Sep 1 range 5.31, Aug 31 range 5.09** — both well *below* ATR of 7.34, while **Aug 28's range of 12.45 was ~1.7× ATR.** Classic expansion → compression sequence.

**Interpretation:** volatility has been transferred from the Aug 27-28 event into a coil. Bollinger squeezes resolve directionally; they do not predict direction. The lens to watch is a **daily close outside 228.92 or 208.63**, ideally on volume >1.2× average (~150M+ shares).

